{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>In my dream, and behold, a vine before me.</b> It is proper for a person to be careful at the beginning of his speech in recounting dreams, for the chief butler — this was the beginning of his speech: 'בחלומי' — in my dream — which is an expression of dreaming and healing, from the language of (Isaiah 38): ״ותחלימני והחייני״ — 'and You have restored me to health and made me live' — and he was delivered and judged for life and returned to his station. But the chief baker began with 'אף' — 'also' — as he said, 'אף אני בחלומי' — 'also I was in my dream' — and he was judged for death." "<b>And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted:</b> Four began with 'אף' and were destroyed from the world. And these are they: the serpent, and the chief baker, and the congregation of Korah, and Haman. And this has already been mentioned in the book of Genesis at the verse (Genesis 3): ״אף כי אמר אלהים״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:19:06.745622210Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}